/*
 * Copyright (c) 2026
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 *
 * The "browser bridge" shield: the peripherals a guest gains when the QEMU
 * machine is qemu-wasm running in a browser tab. It is not a physical shield —
 * the devices are provided by patched QEMU models fed by page JavaScript (see
 * tools/qemu-patches/ and tools/qemu-jit-patches/).
 *
 * Everything lives in the per-machine files under boards/: the devices sit at
 * machine-specific addresses (an MMIO gap on the LM3S6965, a spare PL011 slot
 * on virt), so there is nothing meaningful to add for an arbitrary board.
 *
 * **Every device the shield declares is disabled**, and a snippet named after
 * it turns it on, one row per sample in tools/samples.manifest:
 *
 *   -S gnss          GNSS NMEA receiver on a spare UART (all three machines)
 *   -S host-audio    qemu,host-audio  — I2S out into Web Audio
 *   -S host-mic      qemu,host-mic    — DMIC in from getUserMedia, aliased dmic0
 *   -S host-gpio     qemu,host-gpio + its LED/button nodes (Cortex-M3 only)
 *   -S net           virtio-net, or the Stellaris MAC on the Cortex-M3
 *   -S ramfb         browser-sized qemu,ramfb          (virt machines only)
 *   -S virtio-input  the stock virtio tablet behind it (virt machines only)
 *   -S virtio-gpu / virtio-gpio / virtio-i2c / virtio-spi / virtio-blk
 *                    the virtio-mmio bridges          (virt machines only)
 *
 * The shield's job is to say where each device sits on this machine; which of
 * them an image carries is the manifest's call. That split is what keeps a
 * sample's device dock about the sample: the page grounds the dock in the
 * running build's flattened devicetree (docs/peripherals.md), so a node left
 * okay for convenience is a card the user has to look past.
 *
 * The two boards reach GPIO differently. The Cortex-M3 has qemu,host-gpio, a
 * bespoke register block: fast (a pin read never leaves the guest) but with no
 * interrupt line, so its gpio-keys is polled. The Cortex-A53 has a standard
 * VIRTIO GPIO device, whose driver is stock Zephyr and whose interrupts are
 * real, at the cost of a virtqueue round trip per operation. The LM3S6965 has
 * no virtio-mmio bus, which is why the M3 keeps the MMIO device rather than
 * both boards sharing one.
 */
